<p>A curiosity from DePauw University mathematician Underwood Dudley: Arrange the six permutations of 1, 2, 3 in ascending order and find the differences of successive pairs:</p>
<pre>
123   132   213   231   312  321
    9     81    18    81    9
</pre>
<p>The top row totals 1,332, the bottom row 198. Arrange the first nine digits of &#960; in triplets and add 198 to each:</p>
<pre>
314 159 265
198 198 198
--- --- ---
512 357 463
</pre>
<p>The three sums total 1,332.</p>
<p>(Underwood Dudley, &#8220;What to Do When the Trisector Comes,&#8221; <em>Mathematical Intelligencer</em> 5:1 [March 1983], 20-26, via Martin Gardner&#8217;s <em>Whys &#038; Wherefores</em>, 1989.)</p>
